          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have been anxious for months and now I am not sleeping. How is anxiety assessed, what does guidance describe as first-line, and is the insomnia treated separately?",
          "answer": "The risk check found no emergency pattern in what you described. That does not rule out a problem, so if your symptoms become severe or you feel unsafe, seek medical help promptly.\n\n**How anxiety is assessed:** The generalized anxiety disorder summary (condition-generalized-anxiety-disorder) describes persistent, excessive worry about many everyday matters, occurring more days than not for at least six months, that is hard to control and affects functioning. It also lists common accompanying signs: restlessness, fatigue, poor concentration, irritability, muscle tension and disturbed sleep. The summary does not describe how a clinician makes the assessment, so I cannot give the steps.\n\n**First-line treatment:** The passages I retrieved do not say what guidance describes as first-line treatment for anxiety, so I cannot answer this part. The physical activity summary (lifestyle-generalized-anxiety-disorder-activity) notes that exercise can feel worse before it feels better, because it raises heart rate and breathing in ways that resemble anxiety. That is a point about activity, not a treatment recommendation.\n\n**Is insomnia treated separately?** The retrieved passages do not answer this directly. The insomnia summary (condition-insomnia) explains that short-term insomnia, lasting days to weeks and often with a clear trigger, is common and often resolves. Chronic insomnia disorder is defined as at least three nights a week for at least three months, and it can persist after the original trigger has gone. A coding reference (coding-insomnia-g47) notes that when insomnia is linked to a mental health condition, both are recorded. Whether the sleep problem is handled alongside the anxiety or on its own is not covered in these sources.\n\nGiven how long you have been anxious and the change in your sleep, a doctor or other clinician can assess both and explain your options. Bring a note of when the anxiety started, how often you are sleeping poorly, and how it affects your days.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
